// Runtime/CharacterSetRuntime.maxon — the `CharacterSet` box and its membership test (P1.8 Slice D).
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE `String` TRIMS THAT CONSUME ONE WERE HERE TOO, UNTIL W49 WAVE 4 RETIRED THEM ONTO
// `stdlib/String.maxon`.** What is left is the type the corpus trims still take as a PARAMETER — which is
// the arrangement this file now demonstrates: a compiler-owned type, named by a corpus declaration whose
// own file the corpus never sees, works.
//
// ⭐ **PORTED FROM `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`, WHICH IS THE ORACLE.** Both reference compilers execute that
// same stdlib source — neither has CharacterSet-specific lowering — so there is no divergence between them
// to resolve. What changes is the SHAPE: `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` is not whitelisted, so the type is
// synthesized like `Array`/`Set` and each helper is a hand-built `StdOp` graph.
//
// ⭐ **THE REPRESENTATION IS THE REFERENCE'S, FIELD FOR FIELD** (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:22-24`): a
// `Set with Character` of EXPLICIT members, plus a `categoryMask` of OR'd `General_Category` bits. A
// predefined set carries both (`whitespaces()` is the `Zs` bit *plus* an explicit HT); a
// `CharacterSet.from(…)` carries only the set, with a zero mask that skips the table read entirely.
//
// ⚠⚠ **PLAIN SPACE U+0020 IS NOT AN EXPLICIT MEMBER OF ANY PREDEFINED SET, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.**
// `whitespacesAndNewlines()` seeds only HT/LF/CR/CRLF/VT/FF/NEL (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:36-37`);
// U+0020 is whitespace ONLY through the mask's `Zs` bit. So **even `"  hello  ".trim()` goes through the
// UCD table** — through `__cs_contains`, which the corpus trims call for every cluster they test — — `specs/string-trim.md`'s very first case is exactly that, and the oracle passes it only
// via that lookup. There is a dead `stdlib/Unicode.maxon:3-52` `isWhitespace` with zero callers anywhere
// in the tree and an ASCII-only `stdlib/Ascii.maxon:32-41`; wiring trim to either would be a silent
// divergence from the oracle and would make the whole UCD table look unnecessary. Do not.

// ⭐ **THE BOX'S FIELD OFFSETS AND ITS SIZE** — asserted against the registered `StructLayout`
// (`SignatureIndex.characterSetLayout`) rather than merely agreeing with it by inspection: the type's SHAPE
// is stated for the front end by that layout and written here by these constants, and two readers of one
// shape is the pair this project keeps finding drifted.
export let CharSetCharsOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let CharSetMaskOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
export let CharSetBoxBytes = 16 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE ENTRY POINTS.** All `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (`isCompilerInternalCallee` — no E3004, no
// signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so none widens the heap decision by its prefix; each
// declares its own transitive floor in `recordCallUsage` exactly as the String and set families do.
//
//   * `__cs_make(charsRec, categoryMask)` — mint a `CharacterSet`. THE ONE DOOR the box is born through,
//     so its two fields cannot be written in one order here and another there.
//   * `__cs_decref(csRec)` — the box's DROP. A `CharacterSet` OWNS its member set, so the last owner
//     drops that set before reclaiming the box. The BOX's own drop is a compiler-owned aggregate's
//     compiler-owned one, rather than a synthesized `__destruct_<T>` cascade: the type is not a DECLARED
//     struct, so it is in no `project.structTypes` for `installStructDestructors` to walk. ⚠ The MEMBER SET's
//     drop is a different question and is not this file's to answer — see `characterMemberSetDropCallee`,
//     which is `__set_decref` while `Set` is synthesized and `__destruct_Set_Character` once it is not (W90).
//   * `__cs_contains(csRec, charRec, memberSetLayout, hashWitness, eqWitness)` — the reference's `contains`
//     (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:124-136`): explicit membership first, then the category bit. Its last three
//     arguments are the dictionary-passing block the member probe needs, threaded in by
//     `LowerMaxonToStd.withKeyWitnessArgs` at the tier that can register their `.rdata` blobs.
//
// ⚠ **A FIFTH, `__str_trim`, STOOD HERE UNTIL W49 WAVE 4.** It served all three reference trims
// (`stdlib/String.maxon:714-772`) behind two end flags. They are the corpus's own now, so the graph, its
// flags, its default-set lookup and its `RuntimeUsage` bit are all gone rather than unreferenced.
export let CharSetMakeName = b"__cs_make"
export let CharSetDecrefName = b"__cs_decref"
export let CharSetContainsName = b"__cs_contains"

// `__cs_contains`'s arity, stated once because the BODY declares it and the SUPPLY side of the threading
// must fill it: `[csRec, charRec, memberSetLayout, hashWitness, eqWitness]`. It is FIXED — the descriptor
// rides whether or not the probe forwards it (W90) — so the one thing that can be wrong is which of the five
// the probe passes on, and that is `characterMemberSetProbeArgs`' single read of the fixpoint.
let CharSetContainsParamCount = 5

// The mask value that means "this set has NO category members" — `CharacterSet.from(…)`'s mask
// (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:120`). It is what lets a purely explicit set skip the codepoint decode and
// the table read altogether, which is the reference's own short-circuit (`:128`). Exported because the
// PARSER stamps it into a `from`-built box and this graph tests it — one number, two readers.
export let CharSetNoCategories = 0

// How a `Set with Character` is SPELLED in a diagnostic — for the one message that has to say what
// `CharacterSet.from` wanted. `typeTagName` cannot say it (a generic instance's tag names no arguments),
// and the type has no user-visible alias in shv2 beyond the builtin `CharSet` one.
export let CharacterMemberSetDisplayName = b"Set with Character"

// The low bit a category test isolates: `(categoryMask shr cat) and 1` (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:130`).
let CharSetCategoryBit = 1

// ============================================================================================
// THE ELEVEN PREDEFINED SETS — data, transcribed from `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:31-116`
// ============================================================================================
//
// ⭐ **EACH PRESET IS ITS ACCESSOR NAME, ITS CATEGORY BITMASK AND ITS EXPLICIT MEMBERS**, and it is DATA
// rather than eleven hand-written emitters: they differ in nothing else, and eleven copies of one
// construction is eleven chances to pair a mask with the wrong seed list. The parser walks this table.
//
// ⚠ **THE MASKS ARE THE REFERENCE'S OWN NUMBERS AND THEY ARE NOT RE-DERIVED HERE.** Each is an OR of
// `1 shl <General_Category code>` over the categories `specs/unicode-category.md:19-31` assigns the
// accessor, with the codes fixed by the generated table (`stdlib/helpers/string/unicodeCategory.maxon:9-37`)
// — the same table `__ucd_cat` reads. Recomputing them from a second transcription of that legend would be
// the category codes written down twice.
//
// ⚠ **SEVERAL SEED LISTS LOOK REDUNDANT AND ARE KEPT ANYWAY** — `decimalDigits` seeds `'0'`, which its own
// `Nd` bit already covers. They are the reference's, and dropping one would be a behaviour change argued
// from this compiler's reading of the UCD rather than from the oracle.
export type CharacterSetPreset
	export var method as ByteArray
	export var categoryMask as ParsedInt
	export var members as ByteArrayArray

	static function of(method ByteArray, categoryMask ParsedInt, members ByteArrayArray) returns CharacterSetPreset
		return Self{method: method, categoryMask: categoryMask, members: members}
	end 'of'
end 'CharacterSetPreset'

export typealias CharacterSetPresetArray = Array with CharacterSetPreset

// The explicit members, by the UTF-8 BYTES each character literal in the reference decodes to. Named
// rather than spelled as bare byte arrays at eleven use sites, and spelled as bytes rather than as source
// escapes because this table IS the decode — there is no literal here for a decoder to read.
let HorizontalTabBytes = b"\t"
let LineFeedBytes = b"\n"
let CarriageReturnBytes = b"\r"

// CR+LF is ONE grapheme under UAX#29 GB3, and it is an explicit member in its own right — which is
// precisely why a trim walks CLUSTERS: a byte walk would meet the CR alone and the LF alone. The corpus
// `scanTrimStart`/`scanTrimEnd` do exactly that (`stdlib/String.maxon:674-711`).
let CarriageReturnLineFeedBytes = b"\r\n"
let VerticalTabBytes = b"\x0B"
let FormFeedBytes = b"\x0C"

// U+0085 NEL, in UTF-8. The one seed that is not a single byte apart from CRLF.
let NextLineBytes = b"\xC2\x85"
let DigitZeroBytes = b"0"
let LowerABytes = b"a"
let UpperABytes = b"A"
let ExclamationBytes = b"!"
let DollarBytes = b"$"
let NulBytes = b"\x00"

// The ELEVEN accessor NAMES, which are also what a source `CharacterSet.<name>()` spells. The twelfth
// static's name is NOT here: `from` is a name more than one container spells (`String.from` too), so it is
// `Parser.FromMethod` under that file's rule — *"a method NAME is a token spelling, not a container's
// property"* — where `count`, `append`, `isEmpty`, `clone` and the `Set` family already went (A3g). A
// `CharSet`-prefixed twin of it was the one-fact-written-twice this list's own neighbours were consolidated
// to remove, and its only reader was ever `Parser.maxon`.
export let CharSetWhitespacesMethod = b"whitespaces"
export let CharSetNewlinesMethod = b"newlines"
export let CharSetWhitespacesAndNewlinesMethod = b"whitespacesAndNewlines"
export let CharSetDecimalDigitsMethod = b"decimalDigits"
export let CharSetLowercaseLettersMethod = b"lowercaseLetters"
export let CharSetUppercaseLettersMethod = b"uppercaseLetters"
export let CharSetLettersMethod = b"letters"
export let CharSetAlphanumericsMethod = b"alphanumerics"
export let CharSetPunctuationMethod = b"punctuation"
export let CharSetSymbolsMethod = b"symbols"
export let CharSetControlCharactersMethod = b"controlCharacters"

// The eleven presets, in the reference's declaration order.
//
// ⚠ **EACH MASK CARRIES THE CATEGORIES IT IS, BECAUSE A BARE `520192` CANNOT BE CHECKED AGAINST THE PROSE
// AND THE PROSE IS THE OTHER COPY.** `specs/unicode-category.md:19-31` states the accessor → category
// mapping in English and this table states it in binary; nothing MAKES the two agree, so the arithmetic
// that connects them is written down beside each row. The codes are the generated table's
// (`stdlib/helpers/string/unicodeCategory.maxon:9-37`) — `Lu`=1 … `Cs`=29 — and every row below has been
// recomputed from them against both the prose and `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:31-61`.
export function characterSetPresets() returns CharacterSetPresetArray
	var presets = CharacterSetPresetArray.create()
	// Zs(23)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetWhitespacesMethod, categoryMask: 8388608, members: charactersOf(HorizontalTabBytes)))
	// Zl(24)|Zp(25)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetNewlinesMethod, categoryMask: 50331648, members: newlineMembers()))
	// Zs(23)|Zl(24)|Zp(25)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetWhitespacesAndNewlinesMethod, categoryMask: 58720256, members: whitespaceAndNewlineMembers()))
	// Nd(9)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetDecimalDigitsMethod, categoryMask: 512, members: charactersOf(DigitZeroBytes)))
	// Ll(2)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetLowercaseLettersMethod, categoryMask: 4, members: charactersOf(LowerABytes)))
	// Lu(1)|Lt(3)
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetUppercaseLettersMethod, categoryMask: 10, members: charactersOf(UpperABytes)))
	// Lu(1)|Ll(2)|Lt(3)|Lm(4)|Lo(5)|Mn(6)|Mc(7)|Me(8) — the contiguous run 1..8
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetLettersMethod, categoryMask: 510, members: charactersOf(LowerABytes)))
	// letters plus Nd(9)|Nl(10)|No(11) — the contiguous run 1..11
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetAlphanumericsMethod, categoryMask: 4094, members: charactersOf(LowerABytes)))
	// Pc(12)|Pd(13)|Pe(14)|Pf(15)|Pi(16)|Po(17)|Ps(18) — the contiguous run 12..18
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetPunctuationMethod, categoryMask: 520192, members: charactersOf(ExclamationBytes)))
	// Sc(19)|Sk(20)|Sm(21)|So(22) — the contiguous run 19..22
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetSymbolsMethod, categoryMask: 7864320, members: charactersOf(DollarBytes)))
	// Cc(26)|Cf(27) — and deliberately NOT Co(28)/Cs(29), which the prose omits
	presets.push(CharacterSetPreset.of(CharSetControlCharactersMethod, categoryMask: 201326592, members: charactersOf(NulBytes)))
	return presets
end 'characterSetPresets'

// A one-member list — the shape eight of the eleven presets take.
function charactersOf(only ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	var members = ByteArrayArray.create()
	members.push(only)
	return members
end 'charactersOf'

// `newlines()`'s six: LF, CR, CRLF, VT, FF, NEL (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:34`).
function newlineMembers() returns ByteArrayArray
	var members = ByteArrayArray.create()
	members.push(LineFeedBytes)
	members.push(CarriageReturnBytes)
	members.push(CarriageReturnLineFeedBytes)
	members.push(VerticalTabBytes)
	members.push(FormFeedBytes)
	members.push(NextLineBytes)
	return members
end 'newlineMembers'

// `whitespacesAndNewlines()`'s seven — the newline six with HT in front (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:37`),
// built FROM that list rather than beside it, so the two cannot come to disagree about the six they share.
//
// ⚠⚠ **U+0020 IS NOT IN IT.** See this file's header: plain space is whitespace only through the `Zs` bit,
// which is what puts even `"  hello  ".trim()` through the UCD table.
function whitespaceAndNewlineMembers() returns ByteArrayArray
	var members = charactersOf(HorizontalTabBytes)
	members.append(newlineMembers())
	return members
end 'whitespaceAndNewlineMembers'

// Install the `CharacterSet` runtime into the lowered Std module. Called AFTER the pipeline (like
// `installStringRuntime`), so these are appended already-lowered and never re-optimized. Each is installed
// ONLY when referenced — vocabulary does not ship ahead of its consumer.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES `signatures` BECAUSE TWO OF THE THREE BODIES CALL A TYPE THE PROGRAM MAY DECLARE (W90).**
// The member set is a `Set with Character`, and once `stdlib/Set.maxon` is listed that is an ordinary
// declared generic: the drop is a per-instance `__destruct_Set_Character` cascade and the probe is
// `Set.contains` with a dictionary-passing ABI. Both are read off the index rather than spelled here.
export function installCharacterSetRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	if usage.usesCharSetMake 'make'
		buildCharSetMake(module)
	end 'make'
	if usage.usesCharSetDecref 'decref'
		buildCharSetDecref(module, signatures: signatures)
	end 'decref'
	if usage.usesCharSetContains 'contains'
		buildCharSetContains(module, signatures: signatures)
	end 'contains'
end 'installCharacterSetRuntime'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `__cs_decref`'s BODY WILL CALL, DECLARED BEFORE THE CASCADE FIXPOINT RUNS (W90).** The box owns
// its member set, so the drop names that set's drop callee from inside a SYNTHESIZED Std body — which the
// Maxon-module scan cannot read. Under a retired `Set` that callee is a per-instance
// `__destruct_Set_Character`, and a cascade nobody rooted is never synthesized: the program then dies at
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__destruct_Set_Character'`, with no diagnostic and no line.
// `declareAsyncReleaserNeeds` states the identical obligation for the identical reason, and this is called
// from the same seam, ahead of `noteDestructorUsage`, so the fixpoint closes over the cascade's own fields.
//
// ⚠ It routes through `recordCallUsage` — the same routine the module scan runs over every call op — so this
// declares exactly what a hand-written call to the same callee would, and a new drop family needs no arm here.
export function declareCharacterSetRuntimeNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	if not usesCharacterSets(usage) 'noCharacterSetAtAll'
		return
	end 'noCharacterSetAtAll'

	recordCallUsage(characterMemberSetDropCallee(signatures), usage: usage)
end 'declareCharacterSetRuntimeNeeds'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS PROGRAM HOLD A `CharacterSet` AT ALL — the gate the declaration above needs, and
// DELIBERATELY NOT `usesCharSetDecref` ALONE (W90).** That bit is not settled yet at the seam this must run
// at: a box held in a struct field or a union payload is dropped by a SYNTHESIZED cascade, and it is
// `declareDestructorCascadeNeeds` — inside the very `noteDestructorUsage` this must precede — that turns the
// bit on. MEASURED: gated on `usesCharSetDecref`, `character-set`'s `characterset-at-a-union-payload` and
// `characterset-at-a-generic-instance-field` died at
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__destruct_Set_Character'`.
//
// A box that is MADE or PROBED is one whose members must eventually be reclaimed, so the family's presence is
// the exact question; and the only cost of an over-root is one leaf cascade the program never calls, which
// dead-function elimination then drops. That asymmetry — a missing root is an unpositioned backend panic, an
// extra one is nothing — is the same trade `instanceMentionsTypeParameter` states for its own walk.
function usesCharacterSets(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesCharSetMake or usage.usesCharSetDecref or usage.usesCharSetContains
end 'usesCharacterSets'

// ⭐ **WHO RECLAIMS THE MEMBER SET — the compiler's own hash-table teardown, or the corpus instance's
// `__destruct_<mangled>` cascade (W90).** ONE expression answers both, because `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`
// is already the door every owner of a `Set with Character` asks; the same call is what a scope-exit drop of
// a `CharSet from […]` temporary resolves through (`Parser.decrefCalleeFor`), so the box's drop and a
// binding's drop cannot come to name two different functions.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS READ TWICE — here, to ROOT it, and again to EMIT it — and never cached between**, which is what
// makes the rooting and the emission one answer rather than two that agree by hand.
function characterMemberSetDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns ByteArray
	return signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(signatures.setInstanceForCharacter())
end 'characterMemberSetDropCallee'

// ⭐ **WHO PROBES THE MEMBER SET — `__set_contains`, or the corpus `Set.contains` (W90).** The corpus name is
// asked of the index rather than spelled as a constant: a member declaration is re-keyed by two contests — a
// `static` member wearing an instance member's name (`memberRegistrationKey`) and, in the parser, an overload
// contest that appends `#<paramTypeSpelling>`. `Set` contests neither TODAY, which is exactly what would make
// a literal `b"Set.contains"` rot in silence: the wrong name is not a diagnostic, it is
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function`.
export function characterMemberSetContainsCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns ByteArray
	if signatures.isSetBaseName(SetBuiltinBaseName) 'synthesizedRecord'
		return SetContainsName
	end 'synthesizedRecord'

	return signatures.memberRegistrationKey(qualifiedMethodName(SetBuiltinBaseName, methodName: ContainsMethod), takesReceiver: true)
end 'characterMemberSetContainsCallee'

// The probe's ARGUMENT LIST, which is the half a wrong answer does not panic over: a right name with a wrong
// arity leaves the callee's `param` ops reading whatever the ABI registers held
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.withWitnessTableArgs` records the measured `__map_get` access violation).
//
// The synthesized entry takes `[set, key, hashWitness, eqWitness]`. The corpus method is dictionary-passing,
// so it takes `[user][layout][witness_0..k]` — and whether it carries the LAYOUT at all is a per-METHOD
// verdict of the whole-program fixpoint (MEASURED on this tree: `Set.contains` and `Set.insert` take five
// arguments with the descriptor at index 2, `Set.create` three with it at index 0, and `Set.count` three with
// none). It is READ, never assumed, so a change to `stdlib/Set.maxon`'s body moves this list with it.
function characterMemberSetProbeArgs(signatures ProgramSignatures, chars ValueId, character ValueId, memberSetLayout ValueId, hashWitness ValueId, eqWitness ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	if signatures.isSetBaseName(SetBuiltinBaseName) 'synthesizedRecord'
		return fourArgs(chars, second: character, third: hashWitness, fourth: eqWitness)
	end 'synthesizedRecord'

	var args = pairArgs(chars, second: character)
	if signatures.functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor(characterMemberSetContainsCallee(signatures)) 'carriesTheDescriptor'
		args.push(memberSetLayout)
	end 'carriesTheDescriptor'
	args.push(hashWitness)
	args.push(eqWitness)
	return args
end 'characterMemberSetProbeArgs'

// `__cs_make(charsRec, categoryMask) -> CharacterSet` — one `__mm_alloc` of `CharSetBoxBytes`, then the two
// fields. The destructor slot is the shared `TrivialDestructor`: shv2's drops are STATIC-dispatch (`__mm_free`
// never calls the header pointer), so a `CharacterSet` is dropped by the callee the drop SITE names —
// `__cs_decref`, which `managedNameDropCallee` answers for this type.
function buildCharSetMake(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CharSetMakeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let chars = 0 as ValueId
	let categoryMask = 1 as ValueId

	let size = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: CharSetBoxBytes, ids: ids)
	let dtor = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: TrivialDestructor, ids: ids)
	let box = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(box, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(size, second: dtor)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: CharSetCharsOffset, value: chars)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: CharSetMaskOffset, value: categoryMask)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(box))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCharSetMake'

// `__cs_decref(csRec)` — one fewer owner; at the last, drop the member set and reclaim the box. The
// refcount read-modify-write is the SHARED `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner`, so this box counts owners exactly
// as every other managed box does; only the last-owner path is this function's own.
//
// The member set needs no null guard, unlike a struct cascade's field: `chars@0` is written by `__cs_make`
// from a set the parser has just built, and nothing can move it out — a `CharacterSet` has no field access
// and no mutator (the reference's fields are unexported and immutable, `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:23-24`).
function buildCharSetDecref(module StdModule, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CharSetDecrefName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: box, lastOwner: release, ids: ids)

	let chars = emitLoadField(module, block: release, record: box, offset: CharSetCharsOffset, ids: ids)
	let dropped = ids.mint()
	// A per-instance destructor cascade takes the box and nothing else, exactly as `__set_decref` does, so
	// the ONE thing that moves with the retirement is the NAME — see `characterMemberSetDropCallee`.
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.call(dropped, callee: characterMemberSetDropCallee(signatures), args: argsOf(chars)))
	let freed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.call(freed, callee: MmFreeName, args: argsOf(box)))
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCharSetDecref'

// ⭐⭐ **`__cs_contains(csRec, charRec, memberSetLayout, hashWitness, eqWitness) -> bool` — the reference's
// `contains` (`stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:124-136`), rule for rule.**
//
// Explicit membership FIRST, so a set with no categories never decodes a codepoint; then, only when the
// mask is non-zero, the character's first codepoint is classified and its category bit tested. Both
// short-circuits are the reference's own.
//
// It BORROWS both records: the member probe reads the set and the classifier reads the character's bytes,
// and neither is freed here. The two witness tables are the KEY's `Hashable`/`Equatable` — threaded in by
// `LowerMaxonToStd.appendSetWitnessArgs`, whose key type for this callee is statically `Character` — and are
// forwarded verbatim to the probe, as is the member set's LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR beside them (W90). All three are
// materialized at the MAXON tier, which is what makes their `.rdata` blobs exist: a Std-only builder can
// spell a label but cannot register one (`LowerMaxonToStd.withMemberSetLayoutArg`).
function buildCharSetContains(module StdModule, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CharSetContainsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i1), paramCount: CharSetContainsParamCount, paramTypes: fiveI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(CharSetContainsParamCount)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkMask = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkMask")
	var classify = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classify")
	var present = module.addBlock(func, label: b"present")
	var absent = module.addBlock(func, label: b"absent")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: CharSetContainsParamCount)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let character = 1 as ValueId
	let memberSetLayout = 2 as ValueId
	let hashWitness = 3 as ValueId
	let eqWitness = 4 as ValueId

	let chars = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: CharSetCharsOffset, ids: ids)
	let explicit = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(explicit, callee: characterMemberSetContainsCallee(signatures), args: characterMemberSetProbeArgs(signatures, chars: chars, character: character, memberSetLayout: memberSetLayout, hashWitness: hashWitness, eqWitness: eqWitness)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(explicit, thenBlockId: present.id, elseBlockId: checkMask.id))

	let mask = emitLoadField(module, block: checkMask, record: box, offset: CharSetMaskOffset, ids: ids)
	let noCategories = emitConst(module, block: checkMask, value: CharSetNoCategories, ids: ids)
	let hasCategories = emitCmp(module, block: checkMask, a: mask, b: noCategories, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkMask, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasCategories, thenBlockId: classify.id, elseBlockId: absent.id))

	// The character's FIRST codepoint decides its category — a cluster of several codepoints is classified
	// by its base, which is what `Character.codepoint()` returns (`stdlib/Character.maxon:127-146`) and what
	// the reference passes here (`:129`).
	// ⭐ **THE CORPUS DECODER TAKES THE RECORD AND A POSITION, WHICH IS WHY NO BUFFER ADDRESS IS LOADED HERE
	// ANY MORE.** A `Character` IS a String record, so its first codepoint is `utf8DecodeAt(character, 0)` —
	// exactly what `Character.codepoint()` is in the corpus (`stdlib/Character.maxon:127-146`). The
	// buffer-end bound the retired `__utf8_cp_at` had to be handed explicitly is now the record's own
	// `byteLength()`, read inside the decoder: a lead byte announcing more than the cluster holds decodes as
	// itself rather than reading past (`utf8WidthFits`).
	let firstCodepoint = emitConst(module, block: classify, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let codepoint = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(classify, op: StdOp.call(codepoint, callee: CorpusUtf8DecodeAt, args: twoArgs(character, second: firstCodepoint)))
	let category = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(classify, op: StdOp.call(category, callee: UcdCategoryName, args: argsOf(codepoint)))
	let shifted = emitBin(module, block: classify, a: mask, b: category, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let bit = emitBin(module, block: classify, a: shifted, b: emitConst(module, block: classify, value: CharSetCategoryBit, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let inCategory = emitCmp(module, block: classify, a: bit, b: emitConst(module, block: classify, value: CharSetCategoryBit, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(classify, op: StdOp.condBranch(inCategory, thenBlockId: present.id, elseBlockId: absent.id))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: present, value: PredicateTrue, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: absent, value: PredicateFalse, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCharSetContains'

// ⚠⚠ **`__str_trim` STOOD HERE AND IS THE CORPUS'S NOW (W49 wave 4).** One graph and two end flags
// served all three reference trims (`stdlib/String.maxon:714-772`); with them retired it had no caller,
// and `emitTrimResult`/`emitSelected` had no second one. Two facts from it are worth keeping, because
// they are what the retirement BUYS and what it COSTS, and neither is visible from the corpus:
//
//   * **IT WAS FORWARD-ONLY, SO `trim`/`trimEnd` WERE O(WHOLE STRING)** — measured ×2.00 per doubling out
//     to 524,288 bytes. The corpus `scanTrimEnd` (`stdlib/String.maxon:700`) walks BACKWARD through
//     `findGraphemeStart`, so `trimEnd` is O(trimmed suffix). Retiring is an asymptotic WIN, not a cost.
//   * **THE PRICE IS THE SECOND SEGMENTER shv2 REFUSED TO PORT.** `findGraphemeStart` is a separate
//     implementation of UAX#29 with **no GB12/GB13**, so a regional-indicator pair that `findGraphemeEnd`
//     joins into one flag it splits into two. shv2 had ONE segmenter, forward, and derived both bounds
//     from it precisely to avoid answering "where does this cluster begin?" two ways. Serving the trims
//     from the corpus adopts the backward walk — which is what BOTH reference compilers already execute,
//     so this is convergence on the oracle rather than a divergence from it. It is observable only when
//     the trimmed SET itself holds a regional indicator; `string-trim.md` pins the boundary case.
